</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These perturbations are minimal, as they preserve the core reasoning structure while only altering the final prediction in the incorrect variant. We then combine the prompt <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> with the modified first reasoning step <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and input it to the model as <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}(p\mathrm{{&lt;}think{&gt;}}t^{\prime}_{1}\mathrm{Alternatively})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>think</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Alternatively</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}(p\mathrm{{&lt;}think{&gt;}}t^{\prime}_{1}\mathrm{Alternatively})</annotation></semantics></math> to assess subsequent reasoning behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Result.</span> As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.3 Minor perturbation to the correct first step leads to significant loss ‣ 3 Lost at the beginning of reasoning ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we make two key observations:
(1) Smaller models rarely reach 100% accuracy even when the first reasoning step is correct, suggesting that they may revise or deviate from their initial reasoning. In contrast, larger models (e.g., DS-R1-32B) consistently achieve 100% accuracy given a correct first step, indicating greater stability.
(2) There is a substantial drop in accuracy when the first reasoning step is incorrect, highlighting that even minor errors early in the reasoning process can significantly affect the final prediction. These findings further indicate that the LLM’s ability to self-correct has been overestimated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we observe that the reasoning model is particularly vulnerable at the initial stage of the reasoning process; an error in the first step can propagate and substantially degrade the final prediction. <span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Can we develop a method to identify and retain more promising first reasoning steps while discarding suboptimal ones to enhance the overall generation efficiency?</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Early pruning with hint from first step</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we propose an efficient and straightforward sampling method to identify a promising first reasoning step. By doing so, we can terminate the generation process early when a suboptimal first step is detected, thereby reducing unnecessary computational overhead.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Problem definition</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast to the notation introduced in §<a href="#S3" title="3 Lost at the beginning of reasoning ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we incorporate a random seed <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> to introduce stochasticity into the sampling process. Specifically, a sampled trace is computed as <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\oplus c_{T}=\mathcal{M}(p,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\oplus c_{T}=\mathcal{M}(p,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>. By varying the random seed <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain diverse generations, denoted as <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{n}\oplus c^{n}_{T}=\mathcal{M}(p,\epsilon^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{n}\oplus c^{n}_{T}=\mathcal{M}(p,\epsilon^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{n}=[t^{n}_{1},t^{n}_{2},\ldots,t^{n}_{T}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{n}=[t^{n}_{1},t^{n}_{2},\ldots,t^{n}_{T}]</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
            In prior experiments, we sampled 64 CoT traces per question using 64 distinct random seeds <math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\epsilon^{n}\}_{n=1}^{64}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>64</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\epsilon^{n}\}_{n=1}^{64}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A widely adopted technique for reasoning tasks is majority voting or self-consistency generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. To promote exploration of the reasoning space, models are typically sampled at high temperatures, resulting in diverse outputs. Majority voting then serves to aggregate these outputs by selecting the most frequent final prediction. Formally, majority voting over <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> samples is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\text{maj@}K}=\text{mode}(\{q^{n}_{T}\}_{n=1}^{K})\quad\text{where}\quad q^{n}_{T}=g(c^{n}_{T})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mtext>maj@</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>mode</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>where</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\text{maj@}K}=\text{mode}(\{q^{n}_{T}\}_{n=1}^{K})\quad\text{where}\quad q^{n}_{T}=g(c^{n}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, for models generating long CoT traces, majority voting becomes computationally expensive, as it requires sampling <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> complete reasoning paths independently. In this section, we propose a more efficient approach that samples only <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> full traces, where <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M&lt;N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M&lt;N</annotation></semantics></math>, while maintaining comparable majority voting performance to the one with <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> samplings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Methodology</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In §<a href="#S3" title="3 Lost at the beginning of reasoning ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we demonstrated a strong positive correlation between the first reasoning step and the final prediction. This observation motivates a method that identifies the top <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> most promising first reasoning steps out of a total of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, and continues generation only for these selected <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> candidates, while discarding the remaining <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N-M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N-M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let a reasoning model generate <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> candidate first reasoning step <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t_{1}^{1},t_{1}^{2},\dots,t_{1}^{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t_{1}^{1},t_{1}^{2},\dots,t_{1}^{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math> from a prompt <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> with different random seeds <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\epsilon^{n}\}_{n=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\epsilon^{n}\}_{n=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the first reasoning step of a full reasoning trajectory. We define a scoring function <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r:t_{1}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r:t_{1}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> that estimates the promise of a first step, e.g., rating from a reward model. We then select the top <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> first steps based on their scores:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\text{top}}=\text{TopM}(\{r(t_{1}^{n})\}_{n=1}^{N})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>top</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>TopM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\text{top}}=\text{TopM}(\{r(t_{1}^{n})\}_{n=1}^{N})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Only the selected <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> first steps <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t_{1}^{n}\mid n\in\mathcal{R}_{\text{top}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>top</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t_{1}^{n}\mid n\in\mathcal{R}_{\text{top}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are used for further multi-step generation. The remaining <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N-M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N-M)</annotation></semantics></math> are discarded. Since the first step typically requires only a small number of tokens to generate, this selection approach efficiently reduces computation by avoiding full sampling for the less promising <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N-M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N-M)</annotation></semantics></math> candidates.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.22058v3/maj.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:537/290;" width="537" height="290"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Majority voting accuracy with different number of samplings for four LLMs.
The vertical dashed line denotes the smallest <math id="S4.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> whose accuracy is equal to or larger than the accuracy of <math id="S4.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=64</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Experiments</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span>
We evaluate five families of reasoning models—DS-R1-Qwen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, Qwen3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, Skywork-OR1 (SW-OR1) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, Magistral <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rastogi et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and GPT-OSS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agarwal et al., 2025</a>)</cite>—on five challenging reasoning benchmarks spanning mathematics, science and programming: AIME24, AIME25 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">MAA Committees, 2025</a>)</cite>, HMMT Feb 2025 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Balunović et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, the GPQA Diamond set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rein et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and LiveCodeBench<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
              
              
              
            Latest release, containing problems from Jan–Apr 2025.</span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jain et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, consisting of 30, 30, 30, 198, and 175 problems, respectively. For decoding, we adopt each model’s recommended temperature and top_p, with a maximum generation length of 32K tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We consider values of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> from the set <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,4,8,16,32,64\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,4,8,16,32,64\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For early pruning, we fix <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=64</annotation></semantics></math> and select the top <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> most promising first-step candidates using reward scores predicted by a process reward model (PRM), Qwen2.5-Math-PRM-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. When <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=64</annotation></semantics></math>, the accuracy is exactly the same as the one for <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=64</annotation></semantics></math>, since all candidates are chosen. For PRM, a “step” is defined as a segment of text ending with “<span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">\n\n</span>”, and each step receives an individual reward score. The trajectory <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{n}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{n}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> contains multiple such steps, and we use the score of its final step as the overall reward, <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(t^{n}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(t^{n}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Notably, using a PRM to score <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{n}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{n}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is really cheap, because <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{n}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{n}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is short, and its computation is similar to generate one token with input <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\oplus t^{n}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\oplus t^{n}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike the definition used in §<a href="#S3" title="3 Lost at the beginning of reasoning ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{n}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{n}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> terminates upon generating the keyword “Alternatively”, we redefine <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{n}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{n}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in this section to have a fixed token length, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{len}(t^{n}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{len}(t^{n}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. The previous definition made it difficult to control the computational budget for generating first steps, as the trigger phrase might not appear or could occur late in the sequence. By fixing the token length, we achieve precise control over the generation budget when sampling <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> candidate first steps. By default, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{len}(t^{n}_{1})=512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{len}(t^{n}_{1})=512</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.1 </span>Main results</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2 Methodology ‣ 4 Early pruning with hint from first step ‣ Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we analyze the performance as <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> varies. We find that selecting the top 16 first reasoning steps from 64 candidates and continuing generation from them achieves accuracy on par with, or even exceeding, conventional sampling with <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=64</annotation></semantics></math>. This trend is consistent across diverse LLMs and benchmarks. Interestingly, for certain cases—such as HMMT on DS-R1-Qwen-1.5B, and AIME24, AIME25, and GPQA on DS-R1-Qwen-32B—using as few as <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math> suffices to match the performance obtained with all 64 samples.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Early pruning accuracy and efficiency. We select <math id="S4.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=16</annotation></semantics></math> first steps with the highest reward scores out of <math id="S4.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=64</annotation></semantics></math> candidate first steps. The number of tokens used for the 48 discarded first steps is also included for the early pruning method. Early pruning maintains the accuracy, even improves sometimes, while only requiring <math id="S4.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;30\%</annotation></semantics></math> original inference budget.</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:397.5pt;height:212.2pt;vertical-align:-103.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-29.8pt,15.9pt) scale(0.869578813897892,0.869578813897892) ;">
<table id="S4.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;" colspan="5"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Maj@K accuracy (%)</span> <math id="S4.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Avg. #Tokens <math id="S4.T2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AIME24</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AIME25</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HMMT</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPQA</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.2.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Avg.</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:2.8pt;padding-right:2.8pt;" rowspan="3"><span id="S4.T2.2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text">DS-R1-Qwen-1.5B</span></td>
